1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,517
Hello everyone, and welcome. We are
QAICON Lab, the Quantum and AI for

2
00:00:04,517 --> 00:00:08,837
Computing, Optimization, and
Networking Lab, at Western University.

3
00:00:09,167 --> 00:00:13,728
<font color="#7FD4E6">And today, we want to find out what makes
a qubit different from a random bit.</font>

4
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:17,612
Let's start with something
familiar: if you flip a

5
00:00:17,612 --> 00:00:19,851
fair coin, what you get is
a random bit.

6
00:00:20,433 --> 00:00:23,533
Flip it again and it's still
random, and even if you flip

7
00:00:23,533 --> 00:00:26,577
it a hundred times, it never
gets any easier to predict.

8
00:00:27,167 --> 00:00:30,841
Now, a qubit behaves differently,
and this is where it gets strange.

9
00:00:31,433 --> 00:00:34,316
There is a gate called H,
and if you apply it once and

10
00:00:34,316 --> 00:00:37,253
then measure the qubit, the
result is a fair coin flip.

11
00:00:37,833 --> 00:00:41,088
But apply it a second time, and
the outcome is certain again.

12
00:00:42,067 --> 00:00:44,666
<font color="#7FD4E6">Wait, so where did
the randomness go?</font>

13
00:00:45,733 --> 00:00:48,664
That's exactly the question,
where the randomness goes,

14
00:00:48,664 --> 00:00:51,269
and the answer is stranger
than you might expect.

15
00:00:51,767 --> 00:00:54,074
So let's begin with a bit
that we're unsure about.

16
00:00:54,667 --> 00:00:57,396
We can describe it with two
numbers, the probability

17
00:00:57,397 --> 00:01:00,126
that it is zero, and the
probability that it is one.

18
00:01:00,700 --> 00:01:03,262
If you stack those two
numbers in a column, like

19
00:01:03,262 --> 00:01:05,879
this, you get what's called
a probability vector.

20
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:10,502
<font color="#7FD4E6">So both numbers are at least zero,
and together they add up to one?</font>

21
00:01:10,833 --> 00:01:16,277
Yes, at least zero and adding up to one,
always. For example, a bit that is zero

22
00:01:16,277 --> 00:01:21,170
seven times out of ten is the vector
zero point seven, zero point three.

23
00:01:21,767 --> 00:01:25,978
Two of these vectors are special:
this one, E zero, means the bit

24
00:01:25,978 --> 00:01:30,255
is certainly zero, and this one,
E one, means it is certainly one.

25
00:01:30,833 --> 00:01:33,852
And every other probability
vector is a mixture of these

26
00:01:33,852 --> 00:01:36,871
two, so a fair coin, for
instance, is just half of each.

27
00:01:37,867 --> 00:01:41,095
Now let's actually do something
to the bit, and take the NOT

28
00:01:41,095 --> 00:01:44,268
gate, which turns a zero into a
one, and a one into a zero.

29
00:01:44,867 --> 00:01:49,016
To write down what an operation like
this does, we use a matrix, and here's

30
00:01:49,016 --> 00:01:52,604
the trick I'd like you to remember:
read it one column at a time.

31
00:01:53,200 --> 00:01:56,960
The first column tells you what happens
to a bit that is certainly zero, and

32
00:01:56,960 --> 00:02:00,068
the second column, what happens to a
bit that is certainly one.

33
00:02:01,167 --> 00:02:03,550
So for NOT, a zero
certainly becomes a

34
00:02:03,550 --> 00:02:06,319
one, which makes the
first column zero, one.

35
00:02:06,667 --> 00:02:12,013
<font color="#7FD4E6">Then a one certainly becomes a zero, so
the second column must be one, zero.</font>

36
00:02:12,333 --> 00:02:16,041
Exactly, one, zero. Now let's
try a noisy channel, which

37
00:02:16,041 --> 00:02:19,749
flips the bit only with some
small probability, epsilon.

38
00:02:20,333 --> 00:02:24,732
Here a zero stays zero with probability
one minus epsilon, and becomes a one

39
00:02:24,732 --> 00:02:28,251
with probability epsilon, and that
gives us the first column.

40
00:02:28,600 --> 00:02:32,405
<font color="#7FD4E6">And in the second column, a one
stays one with probability one</font>

41
00:02:32,405 --> 00:02:35,960
<font color="#7FD4E6">minus epsilon, and flips to
zero with probability epsilon.</font>

42
00:02:36,300 --> 00:02:39,724
Exactly right, so that's
epsilon on top and one minus

43
00:02:39,724 --> 00:02:43,740
epsilon below. Notice that each
column is itself a probability

44
00:02:43,740 --> 00:02:47,756
vector, and matrices like this
are called stochastic matrices.

45
00:02:48,733 --> 00:02:52,749
Here's one more example: suppose we
throw the bit away and just flip a

46
00:02:52,749 --> 00:02:56,357
fair coin instead, so that both
columns are one half, one half.

47
00:02:56,933 --> 00:02:59,736
If you apply that twice,
nothing changes, because a

48
00:02:59,736 --> 00:03:02,596
fair coin that's flipped
again is still a fair coin.

49
00:03:03,567 --> 00:03:07,581
<font color="#7FD4E6">So couldn't one of these stochastic
matrices just do what H does?</font>

50
00:03:09,067 --> 00:03:12,472
Good question, so let's
suppose one of them does. Say

51
00:03:12,472 --> 00:03:15,812
some matrix M turns a certain
zero into a fair coin.

52
00:03:16,400 --> 00:03:19,599
Now we apply M a second time,
at a point where the bit is

53
00:03:19,599 --> 00:03:22,170
zero half of the time, and
one the other half.

54
00:03:22,767 --> 00:03:26,746
And the thing I want you to notice is that
M acts on each of those cases separately.

55
00:03:27,333 --> 00:03:31,110
So follow the first case, where the
bit is zero and M makes it a fair coin

56
00:03:31,110 --> 00:03:34,473
again, which means that one quarter of
the time, we end up at one.

57
00:03:35,067 --> 00:03:39,194
Now for the second case, and here's
the point: whatever M does down here,

58
00:03:39,194 --> 00:03:43,091
it can only add probability, and it
can never take that quarter away.

59
00:03:43,867 --> 00:03:48,711
<font color="#7FD4E6">So outcome one keeps a probability of
at least a quarter, whatever happens.</font>

60
00:03:49,033 --> 00:03:52,744
Right, at least a quarter, and
that means certainty never

61
00:03:52,744 --> 00:03:55,924
comes back. Probabilities add.
They never cancel.

62
00:03:57,600 --> 00:04:00,738
So what does quantum theory
do differently? Surprisingly

63
00:04:00,738 --> 00:04:03,762
little, actually, because it
keeps the same picture, a

64
00:04:03,761 --> 00:04:06,728
column of two numbers that
gets acted on by matrices.

65
00:04:07,300 --> 00:04:10,632
Let's start again from the
fair coin, one half, one half.

66
00:04:11,200 --> 00:04:13,776
And now here is a qubit,
whose entries are one

67
00:04:13,776 --> 00:04:16,181
over root two, and minus
one over root two.

68
00:04:16,967 --> 00:04:20,189
That really is a minus sign,
and no probability vector could

69
00:04:20,189 --> 00:04:23,466
ever look like that. So this is
the first change: the entries

70
00:04:23,466 --> 00:04:26,579
are allowed to be negative, and
they're called amplitudes.

71
00:04:27,167 --> 00:04:31,093
They can even be complex numbers, but
real ones are all we'll need for now.

72
00:04:31,667 --> 00:04:35,904
So what keeps numbers like these in
check? Well, try squaring each one,

73
00:04:35,904 --> 00:04:39,898
and you get one half and one half,
which together make exactly one.

74
00:04:40,467 --> 00:04:42,924
And that's the second
change: it's the squares

75
00:04:42,924 --> 00:04:45,653
that have to total one, not
the numbers themselves.

76
00:04:46,233 --> 00:04:50,244
If you drop the minus sign you get
another qubit, one over root two,

77
00:04:50,244 --> 00:04:54,374
one over root two, and its squares
total one as well. On the sphere we

78
00:04:54,374 --> 00:04:58,145
saw at the start, these two qubits
point in opposite directions.

79
00:04:59,233 --> 00:05:03,030
There's one more rule, and it's the one
that connects amplitudes to what we

80
00:05:03,030 --> 00:05:06,980
actually see: the square of each amplitude
is the probability of that outcome.

81
00:05:07,300 --> 00:05:10,750
<font color="#7FD4E6">So if I measure either of
these, I get zero half the time</font>

82
00:05:10,750 --> 00:05:14,077
<font color="#7FD4E6">and one half the time. They
both look like a fair coin.</font>

83
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:17,896
Exactly, they both look like a
fair coin, and that is what a

84
00:05:17,896 --> 00:05:21,452
single H produces, at least as
far as a measurement can tell.

85
00:05:22,033 --> 00:05:24,509
But look closely at the
two vectors, because

86
00:05:24,509 --> 00:05:27,042
they are not the same:
they differ by a sign.

87
00:05:27,833 --> 00:05:31,945
A measurement can't see that sign,
but a second H can, and it sends the

88
00:05:31,945 --> 00:05:36,115
first vector back to certainly zero,
and the other one to certainly one.

89
00:05:36,700 --> 00:05:39,214
And the reason it works
is that amplitudes can

90
00:05:39,214 --> 00:05:41,895
cancel, a negative number
against a positive one.

91
00:05:43,567 --> 00:05:46,406
So here are the two worlds
we've met, side by side.

92
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:49,838
A random bit is two
probabilities, which are changed

93
00:05:49,838 --> 00:05:53,233
by stochastic matrices, and you
read it out simply by looking.

94
00:05:53,800 --> 00:05:56,614
A qubit is two amplitudes,
which are changed by

95
00:05:56,614 --> 00:05:59,672
unitary matrices, and you
read it out by measuring.

96
00:06:00,267 --> 00:06:03,490
But the puzzle we started with is
still open, because we haven't

97
00:06:03,490 --> 00:06:06,560
yet watched that cancellation
happen between the two H gates.

98
00:06:07,533 --> 00:06:12,107
And that's where we go next, starting
with section one point two, on states.
